  "outcome": "NO_DIFFERENCE_DETECTED",
  "reading": "On 3,265 D. melanogaster brain distal elements called differentially accessible in exactly one of 42 cell types (Janssens et al. 2022, larval to adult brain, the source's own lvl1 call against the rest of the brain) and lying in no protein-coding gene body, the gene with the nearest 5' end inside the element's domain, delimited by 3,121 Hi-C boundaries of nuclear-cycle-14 embryos (Stadler et al. 2017, the authors' manual call), is expressed at 5 counts per million or more and highest in the element's own cell type, in RNA summed over that cell type's cells (Davie et al. 2018, the counterpart cluster the accessibility source itself declares), for 151 of 3,265 (4.6%), against 147 of 3,265 (4.5%) for the gene with the nearest 5' end on the arm; the rules differ on 216 of 3,265 elements; difference +0.12 points, 95% -0.25 to +0.61 over 1-Mb clusters. No difference detected between the boundary rule and nearest TSS. With 216 elements differing, a difference smaller than about 1.3 points could go undetected; section 9 could only bound it at 2.9 points, on 90 differing elements. Against the gene-structure rule on the same elements (150 of 3,265, 4.6%; the two differ on 789, and neither names a host here), the difference is +0.03 points, 95% -0.50 to +0.60: no difference detected between the two nodes. The boundary rule's genes match the element's cell type more often than genes at a matched distance (2.0%, one-sided p 0.001), so position carries cell-type information here. With the same boundaries rotated along each arm the rule averages 4.5% on these elements (one-sided p 0.149), so the measured positions are not shown to carry more than the boundaries' number and spacing. On all 10,052 distal elements (beside, deciding nothing; the rules differ on 869) the boundary rule is concordant for 656 (6.5%) against 662 (6.6%) for nearest TSS, -0.06 points [-0.36, +0.24], and against 882 (8.8%) for the gene-structure rule, -2.25 points [-2.88, -1.66]. 3,911 of the attributed genes on this population are absent from the RNA atlas's rows and are counted as not concordant. Accessibility is not function, and a boundary is not a regulatory unit by itself: this says the attributed gene is expressed where the element is open, not that the element acts on it. The boundaries come from 2-3 h embryos while the elements and the RNA come from larval-to-adult brain, a wider stage gap than section 9's; accessibility and RNA come from different nuclei and different studies; and the element source states no licence.",
